description: The llvm backend should reach let-go on the WasmGC backend's terms, through the same seam and the same let-go issue, rather than as a second proposal with its own seam. This is the llvm lane's account to sit beside git-issue:c2838dd: how far the lane is from let-go proper, what it adds to the seam, and the choices only the maintainers can make.
 
 Where parity stands. The llvm lane holds itself to the GC lane's bars rather than keeping its own: the program corpora the GC lane runs, 271 of 271 (P13.9), with no corpus the GC lane covers left uncovered (P13.15); let-go's core tests at P5.1's bar (P13.12); legmacs' suite and eval tests failing the same tests as the GC lane (P13.13); xsofy's world dump identical to native lg on seeds 1 to 20 (P12.38). The parity branch reaches that on the current pin with one stopgap in the runtime snapshot, which goes when nooga/let-go#1106 lands.
 
 The seam is shared, and the llvm side adds nothing to it. The ten overridden vars all live in the driver and lower_wasm; the llvm lowering's files override none of them. Whichever shape the maintainers choose for the seam, a backend record or the vars declared as the contract, carries both backends, so one let-go issue covering both is the simpler ask.
 
 What the llvm lane adds is its own packaging cost:
 - a compile needs LLVM's clang and lld on the machine, and the host runtime in C (host/native);
 - a warm compile takes 4 to 5 s with the reader fix and 9.8 s without it, against a goal of under 1 s; the plan is git-issue:d2b7f41;
 - the runtime under rt/llvm is a deliberate fork of rt/wasm, to be joined later through a shared core rather than merged.
 
 Packaging, then: the same three shapes, and the same order as git-issue:c2838dd, with the llvm lowering last. It should stay in this repo until the compile-time plan lands, because an in-tree target that needs a full LLVM install and minutes per corpus is not a cost let-go's CI should take on.
 
 The public reasons: native speed for code written in let-go, against the bytecode VM, from docs/benchmarks/llvm-compute-2026-10-16: fib in 627 ms against 1,818 ms, and xxh in 45.6 ms against 3,760 ms. The claim is about let-go code: native lg calling Go's own xxh through interop takes 0.32 ms. Beyond speed, the lane targets 32-bit and bare-metal boards through hardware profiles (P13.5, P13.6), which the VM cannot reach.
 
 Facts that close this: F1, the co-driver's read on folding the llvm lane into git-issue:c2838dd's let-go issue, as a comment here; F2, that issue carrying an llvm section built from this one, linked from here.

